---
/**
 * The landing page: the one page a reader who has not installed anything sees,
 * and the doorway to the other four.
 *
 * The five sections are the order a reader needs them — what it is, what it
 * will not do, the panel it adds, the two places its state lives, what it needs
 * first — and each heading is the spec's own noun rather than one invented for
 * the page (FR-013 – FR-019). Two of them discharge the constitution and not
 * only the specification: the second states that nothing runs while OpenChamber
 * is off and names the Status tab that reports it (AC-030), and the fourth
 * publishes both storage locations with what each holds and which survive an
 * uninstall (AC-031). Nothing here claims or implies a hosted control plane
 * (Principle V): the service that reads GitHub is the reader's own, spawned by
 * their own OpenChamber.
 *
 * **What this page deliberately does not carry.** FR-018's four documentation
 * links and the licence link arrive with `Layout`, so repeating them would be a
 * second list of the same five addresses — and a one-line summary of each
 * documentation page would be a fifth place its subject is described, which is
 * the drift FR-049 exists to prevent. No permission, configuration field,
 * dispatch state or endpoint is named here either: each is a documentation
 * page's subject, and three of the four derive theirs from the shipped
 * declaration rather than from a second copy (FR-048).
 */
import Layout from '../layout.astro';
import { PRODUCT_ID } from '../data/product.ts';
---

<Layout>
    <h1>Mecha Turk</h1>

    <section>
        <h2>What Mecha Turk does</h2>
        <p>
            Mecha Turk turns GitHub work meant for you into OpenChamber agent
            sessions. A local service polls the GitHub repositories you bind to
            it, watching for issues assigned to your account, review requests,
            and mentions. Each match becomes a queued dispatch, and the panel
            turns that dispatch into one session — inside a project you have
            already registered, where OpenChamber builds the worktree and the
            chat.
        </p>
        <p>
            Work travels one way: GitHub activity in, OpenChamber sessions out.
            Everything between those two ends happens on your own machine. Your
            OpenChamber installation runs the panel, spawns the service that
            polls, and holds the state — there is no Mecha Turk server, no
            account to sign in to, and nothing to keep running elsewhere.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>What Mecha Turk will not do</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>It never creates a project or a worktree.</strong> It
                asks OpenChamber for sessions inside projects you registered
                yourself; a binding whose project is missing stops and says so
                rather than making one.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Nothing runs while OpenChamber is off.</strong> Polling
                and dispatch stop when OpenChamber stops, when the extension is
                disabled, or when its service is not running.
                The <strong>Status</strong> tab is where that honest state is
                read: whether the service is up, when it last read, and whether
                what you are in front of is current or stale.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>The only GitHub traffic is that outbound polling.</strong> Nothing
                connects in, there are no webhooks, and the panel itself makes
                no GitHub request of its own.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>It cannot write to GitHub.</strong> It reads issues and
                pull requests; it cannot comment, label, assign, or merge.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>The panel</h2>
        <p>
            Mecha Turk adds one panel to the OpenChamber rail, with six tabs in
            this order.
        </p>
        <dl>
            <dt>Status</dt>
            <dd>
                Whether the service is up and polling, your accounts, your
                bindings, the agent pin, and the setup prerequisites — each with
                its own state and its own remediation.
            </dd>
            <dt>Dispatches</dt>
            <dd>
                Every dispatch queued, started, or finished, with the reason and
                the controls that move it: retry it, resolve it, return it to
                waiting, or read its audit history.
            </dd>
            <dt>Bindings</dt>
            <dd>
                The repositories you watch, the OpenChamber project each one
                dispatches into, and the form that adds another.
            </dd>
            <dt>Accounts</dt>
            <dd>
                The GitHub accounts it polls as, each one's connection state and
                scopes, and the form that adds another.
            </dd>
            <dt>Settings</dt>
            <dd>
                The service's whole configuration, one row per field with its
                bounds and the line saying when a change takes effect.
            </dd>
            <dt>About</dt>
            <dd>
                The name, the version the service reports, where the source
                lives, and read-only diagnostics behind a disclosure.
            </dd>
        </dl>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Where your data lives</h2>
        <p>
            Two locations, and only two. Everything that has to survive is in
            the first one.
        </p>
        <dl>
            <dt><code>~/.config/openchamber/mecha-turk/</code></dt>
            <dd>
                The service's own store: your accounts — including the tokens,
                written as plain text — your bindings, the queued events and
                their dispatches, the audit trail, and the configuration
                document. <strong>It survives an uninstall.</strong> Nothing
                deletes it for you, so remove it yourself when you are done.
            </dd>
            <dt>OpenChamber's extension storage, under <code>{PRODUCT_ID}:</code> keys</dt>
            <dd>
                The panel's own state: the project you picked, the evidence
                record for the last match, its dispatch records, and a redacted
                test ledger. <strong>It is wiped on uninstall</strong>, which is
                why nothing that has to be kept lives there — the audit trail is
                in the folder above, where you control it and can back it up.
            </dd>
        </dl>
        <p>
            Treat that folder the way you treat <code>~/.ssh</code>: back it up
            deliberately, never commit it, and revoke a token on GitHub as soon
            as you no longer need it.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Before you start</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>OpenChamber desktop or web.</strong> The VS Code and
                mobile builds do not spawn a local service, so the extension is
                unsupported there.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>A registered OpenChamber project for every repository
                you bind.</strong> Register it in OpenChamber first; the
                extension cannot create one.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>A personal access token per account</strong>, fine-grained
                preferred, with read access to <code>Metadata</code> and to
                read <code>Issues</code> and <code>Pull requests</code> as well,
                plus <code>Contents</code> if you pass repository metadata to
                OpenChamber, and <strong>no write scopes</strong> at all —
                organization approval where your organization requires it.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Recommended:</strong> pin the Default Agent in
                OpenChamber's session defaults, then set <code>expectedAgent</code> on
                the Settings tab to the same name, so a session that ran under a
                different agent is pointed out to you.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </section>
</Layout>